WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:01.553
- [Jason] Ndamukong Suh

00:00:01.553 --> 00:00:04.050
has been one of the most
feared defenders in the NFL

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:05.963
since the moment he came into the League.

00:00:09.985 --> 00:00:11.040
- [Suh] When I'm on the football field,

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.600
I'm in between those white lines,

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.280
my job is to piss everybody off.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.850
And so if I don't do that,
I'm not doing my job.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:21.540
- [Jason] At 6'4" and
more than 300 pounds,

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.410
Ndamukong Suh has done his job
in the NFL for 12 years now,

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.960
sometimes, maybe too well.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.610
- [Rodgers] He got, you know, frozen feet

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.320
and didn't realize he
was stepping on my calf,

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.540
he also stomped on one of
my teammates at one point.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.430
He's been, you know, in my time,

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.940
one of the most dominant
three techniques in the game.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.800
- [Suh] And I understand not
everybody's gonna like me

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:41.670
and that's okay.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:43.710
But I guarantee the
people that don't like me

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:44.820
don't know who I am,

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.640
and haven't even taken the
time to get to know me.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.440
- [Jason] After spending
a few days with Ndamukong

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.630
here in the rainy city of Portland,

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:52.500
I've gotten an insight

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.290
into a soft-spoken native Portlandian,

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:58.530
family man restaurateur
and real estate investor,

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:01.740
who's endeavoring to have more
impact with his businesses

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.710
than he's had against
quarterbacks in the NFL.

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:06.210
- It's a simple concept of like,

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.950
I don't wanna be at the top of the pyramid

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.380
or be amongst the 1%
and be there by myself.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:13.110
I wanna bring other people
with me that are like-minded

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:14.310
and you wanna work with them.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.000
And when you have the
opportunity to help other people

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.120
at the same time, it's a
smart business decision.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.503
Why not create both?

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:30.293
Dunk.

00:01:37.383 --> 00:01:38.557
- [Katya] Say hello.

00:01:38.557 --> 00:01:40.247
Should we get the basketball
court and basketball-

00:01:40.247 --> 00:01:42.720
- King's is more the observer

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.390
and analyzes before he dives in,

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:49.950
and Khari is full throttle
right into it, right away.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.480
They don't go anywhere without each other.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:52.693
The few times that they have,

00:01:52.693 --> 00:01:55.120
they're trying to figure
out where the other one is.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.080
And I have twin sisters
and I have twin cousins.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.430
Twins definitely run in the family,

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.530
she has twins on her side too.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.870
- [Jason] For someone who is
so single-minded on the field,

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:07.620
seeing Suh in his native
habitat, it's a bit startling,

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.720
and not just because he's a dad.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:10.650
From his home office,

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:13.230
bouncing from Zooms to calls to text,

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.840
he's methodically creating an empire.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.790
It includes investments in tech companies

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:19.170
like Quip and Ember,

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.730
and hospitality investments

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.520
including restaurants
in Portland and beyond.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.760
But the primary focus for
Suh has been real estate.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.650
Over the past decade,
much of his investments

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.743
have been focused on multifamily
and commercial projects.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.300
So tell me your business
philosophy, like in a nutshell,

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:37.770
like how do you distill it down?

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:40.140
- Just understanding that investing

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.260
and social impact can go,
without question, hand in hand.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:46.860
And I think you can impact other
people's lives and do good,

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:47.910
but at the same time,

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.670
make smart business
decisions where it's...

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.310
I've always seen it as a true
give and take relationship

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.650
versus just someone always taking,

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.930
and I think that's where
that capitalistic mindset

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.763
that majority of America lives in.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:07.140
It's a short trip from where we're sitting

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.120
to see where Suh came from.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:10.260
His home in the off season

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.750
is here in the Irvington
district of Portland.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.640
And his house is only a few streets away

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:15.740
from where he grew up.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:19.050
- This hoop's been here for ages.

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:20.010
- [Jason] Oh my gosh.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.500
And the family that owned this house,

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.720
their son actually played in the NBA.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.510
There used to be at the far end,

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:28.860
we called that little hoop

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.350
where we'd have our main dunk contest.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:33.690
- What was Portland like,
like when you were growing up?

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.540
I mean now it's seen as
this like boom town, right?

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.750
But was it like that 20 years ago?

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.930
- [Suh] No, Portland was very, very small.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:44.280
Everybody knew everybody pretty much.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:45.690
- Yeah.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.210
Growing up in Portland, Suh
was surrounded by sports.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.880
Nike's headquarters was
just 20 minutes away

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.040
and he still works out
there when he's home.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.300
His older sister, Ngum Suh,
also became an elite athlete.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.930
She'd end up going to the 2004
Olympics as a soccer player,

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:03.530
playing for their father's
home country of Cameroon.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.240
Oftentimes in a family,

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.000
especially when you have
multiple superstars,

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.730
it's clear that like this
family's in the business

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:13.560
of growing superstar athletes.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.270
- My parents could care less about that.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:16.860
They wanted us to be in athletics

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:21.030
and have that fulfillment
of being in sports.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:24.600
But hands down, education
was the number one.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:25.950
And my sister's super smart,

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:29.400
she got her degree in kinesiology
and biological sciences.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.140
- [Jason] Suh's parents
divorced when he was two,

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:32.460
but stayed close.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.260
His mom was an elementary school teacher

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.720
and his dad owned an HVAC company.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.640
- As a young kid, I enjoyed
going to work with him.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:45.750
I got to spend a lot of time
with my dad on job sites.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:49.830
And it's really what got
me into the business.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:52.500
As I got a little bit older
towards middle school,

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.117
it would be me going to the
job site and being able to say,

00:04:55.117 --> 00:05:00.117
"Alright, you can earn 5, 10
dollars towards your bike."

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:01.560
He just wanted to see

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.780
if I'd be willing to get up
at six o'clock in the morning.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:04.740
- Right.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:06.660
- [Suh] This was my dad's house
that where you grew up in.

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:08.220
Where he bought the building,

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:10.740
lived on one side and
rented the other side.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:12.581
And that's where we
really started to learn

00:05:12.581 --> 00:05:13.414
about real estate

00:05:13.414 --> 00:05:15.900
or how I started to
learn about real estate.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:16.860
- [Jason] From those early days.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.860
Suh's fascination with and
ambition in real estate

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:22.770
only grew, especially
as a pro football career

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:25.950
gave him the means to eye
bigger and bigger projects.

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:27.390
He's been buying and building property

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:28.620
in his hometown of Portland

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.810
as well as across the United States,

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:34.320
including luxury residential,
office and hospitality space,

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.000
and affordable housing,

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:38.400
all with the mission to help
build generational wealth

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.020
through real estate.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.690
And real estate is one of the
most tried and true places

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:44.700
to store and grow wealth.

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:47.670
Buying and owning property,
commercial or residential,

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:48.503
when done right,

00:05:48.503 --> 00:05:52.440
can be a source of income as
well as accrue value over time.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:55.890
That's if you choose projects
wisely and have patience.

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:57.090
Over the past few years,

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:59.070
the pandemic both increased demand

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:01.050
and lowered the supply of housing

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:03.330
leading to skyrocketing values.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:04.710
At the same time,

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:07.260
low interest rates made
acquiring the capital

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:10.650
needed to purchase properties even easier.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:12.150
More recently though,

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.250
rising mortgage rates and new supply

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:16.110
have begun to change that picture

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:18.723
and rents have been
increasing at historic levels.

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:21.900
Owning real estate has
the additional benefit

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:25.380
of influencing, maybe even transforming,

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.750
the neighborhood and the
community around you.

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:30.675
That's an idea passed down
to Suh from his father.

00:06:30.675 --> 00:06:32.190
- Hey.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:35.100
- He assembled a handful of
different lots and projects,

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.920
and this is one of them that has been,

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:41.550
I won't say a unicorn, but has
been very, very profitable.

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:44.340
If you look at the building,
you had the exterior.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:48.240
So we gutted everything inside
and kept the exterior facade

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.400
and then built on top of that.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:53.490
It was originally a chop
shop when we first took over.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:54.323
People at night,

00:06:54.323 --> 00:06:56.640
when I was growing up in
the 90s and early 2000s

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:58.890
would not walk up and down
these streets by any means.

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:00.060
And you see it's live,

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:02.490
you got great restaurants up and down,

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:03.960
coffee shops further down.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:06.090
You got, everybody probably
knows the Salt &amp; Straw,

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:07.170
they're right up the street.
- Right.

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:09.360
- There's no way they would
be here back in the 90s.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:12.270
So my dad had a great vision in focus

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.343
for seeing where the city was moving.

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:17.730
- [Jason] Athletes investing
in real estate properties

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:21.420
in order to renovate them isn't
that remarkable at its face.

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:23.460
What separates Suh is his fluency

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:25.410
in the details of the business.

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.480
He learned that on the job with his dad

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:29.520
as well as in the classroom.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.170
- [Sports Narrator] Account for him,

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:34.200
plan for him, recognize him,

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:36.540
as perhaps the most
disruptive defensive player

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:37.740
in college football.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:39.900
- He's the most dominant player
that I've seen this season.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:41.190
- [Jason] A heavily recruited athlete,

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:43.380
he opted for the University of Nebraska,

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:45.480
which has not only a great football team,

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:48.840
but a highly regarded
construction engineering program.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:50.850
- I knew leaving high school,

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:52.620
I wanted to get an engineering degree.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:53.580
And because my ultimate goal

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:55.440
was to come back here to Portland

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:58.950
and build another division
to my dad's company.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:00.510
Granted that didn't necessarily happen

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:03.033
because other things transpired, but like-

00:08:03.033 --> 00:08:06.790
- Like going pro.
- Yeah, like going pro, yeah.

00:08:08.053 --> 00:08:10.740
I think I get it from both my
parents, but primarily my dad.

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:14.370
We've always been somewhat
calculated and focused

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:16.920
on what we wanna accomplish in building

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:18.390
and connecting with the right people.

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:20.390
And then also just learning from others.

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:23.280
- Lincoln, Nebraska may
not be the first place

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:24.113
you'd think to meet

00:08:24.113 --> 00:08:26.730
some of the world's most
influential business people.

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:29.910
The Portland Metro area alone
has more people living in it

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:32.460
than the entire state of Nebraska.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:34.740
But his play on the
field allowed him to meet

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:36.990
and maintain relationships
with future mentors

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:38.430
in his business career,

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:42.810
including the Oracle of Omaha
himself, Warren Buffett.

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:45.330
- I loved the Wall Street
Journal story last year

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:47.931
that called you a secret finance nerd.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:51.180
Let's talk a little
bit about that because-

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:52.740
- This guy is smart, believe me.

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:53.573
- You're not somebody-

00:08:53.573 --> 00:08:56.610
- [Suh] Ever since then,
we've been close friends,

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:58.920
confidant, and really a true mentor,

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:01.050
being able to bounce a bunch
of different ideas off,

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:04.110
and then also understand
how he envisions the world.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:05.910
Although he's not fully into real estate

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:07.260
and he's changed a little bit

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:10.317
'cause he now has Berkshire
Hathaway Homes and whatnot.

00:09:10.317 --> 00:09:12.300
- I'm gonna end this.
- [Commentator] Down goes Suh.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:13.530
- [Suh] That long term vision

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:15.810
in that being able to buy and hold

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:20.100
is one of the greatest things
ever that I learned from him.

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:20.933
- [Jason] Suh also met

00:09:20.933 --> 00:09:22.440
with one of the most interesting people

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:24.150
who has split time between the locker room

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:26.730
and the boardroom, Joe Moglia.

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:29.100
Moglia's career began
in the coaching world,

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:31.890
but he would rise through the
ranks to lead TD Ameritrade

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:35.010
as CEO from 2001 to 2008,

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:37.920
and chairman from 2008 to 2020,

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:40.620
navigating the company through
the global financial crisis

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:41.880
and its aftermath,

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:45.030
as well as its eventual
combination with Schwab.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:47.460
- He was around my entire senior year

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:50.377
and one day I just asked him, he's like,

00:09:50.377 --> 00:09:53.190
"You always leave every
Friday before our games

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:55.320
and come back Saturdays, ready to go.

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:56.190
Like where do you go?"

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:58.080
And it's always like
a black car going out.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:09:58.980
He's like, "I go to New York,

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:00.780
I go take care of business
with TD Ameritrade,

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:01.680
I'm the chairman there,

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:03.420
and then I come back and obviously

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:04.800
have my role here with you guys."

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:06.180
And I was like, wow,
that's pretty interesting.

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:07.590
Like I'd love to learn more.

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:08.707
And so he said,

00:10:08.707 --> 00:10:12.060
"Well, make sure you follow
up with me after the season."

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:15.300
- Alright so I asked him
this exact same question,

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:17.160
check out what Joe had to say.

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:20.640
- So our first step, our
first step is going to be,

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:22.800
I want you to come down to my office.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:23.970
The season was over

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:26.790
and I wanna see the budget
that you would put together.

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:28.800
So he comes down, it's
about a month later,

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:30.300
and I gotta tell you, I was impressed.

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:32.760
He comes into my office
with a suit on, tie on.

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:34.110
He looked great.

00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:36.150
And we sat down, he showed me his budget.

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:37.920
The budget was about $60,000.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:39.960
Now we've talked about that a little bit.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:41.110
In two or three months,

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:44.400
he was gonna get a pretty
significant contract

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:46.500
and he was gonna make a shitload of money,

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:48.570
and a $60,000 budget

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:50.460
with regards to all the different things

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:52.140
that the people's gonna
be interacting with

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:54.690
is probably not gonna quite work out.

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:56.577
- Yeah, that's exactly right.

00:10:56.577 --> 00:10:58.680
He's like, "Man, you're gonna be spending

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:02.280
anywhere from 20, 30,000 dollars a month."

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:05.100
And I was like there's no
way, I'm in college right now.

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:07.110
My rent's like 1,500.

00:11:07.110 --> 00:11:09.240
Yeah, I didn't imagine ever getting there.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:10.773
Lo and behold, I'm there so.

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:14.820
- [Jason] There was of course
one other pivotal person

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:16.830
he'd meet and cultivate
a relationship with

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:20.100
during his college days,
his future wife, Katya.

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:21.930
- I have only been a champion

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:24.960
with this beautiful lady in my life.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:27.270
- [Katya] We met all the way back in 2009.

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:29.340
I was a women's basketball player

00:11:29.340 --> 00:11:31.140
and he was a football player.

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:34.200
And the best way that
I like to explain it is

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:36.570
he ran after me a lot,

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:39.540
and would always try to kinda
say he's gonna break my ankles

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:40.830
or when are we gonna play one-on-one?

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:43.890
And I was like, "Hi, who are you?"

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:47.130
And so after some time I
finally gave him a little,

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:48.750
a little chance and I got to know him,

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:51.873
and truly he became my best friend.

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:55.800
- [Jason] The two have had
a whirlwind past few years.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:57.600
They moved in together in 2018,

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.370
were engaged in 2019,

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:03.570
and had their then traditional
Zoom wedding back in 2020.

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:06.240
In February of 2021, Ndamukong would win

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:08.910
his first Super Bowl with
the Tampa Bay Buccaneers,

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:10.530
and just a few months later,

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:14.190
the two would welcome their
twin sons, Khari and Kingston.

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:15.690
When the two aren't changing diapers

00:12:15.690 --> 00:12:17.640
or doing the latest TikTok trend,

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:19.080
they're business partners,

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:20.630
working together out
of the same home office

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:22.620
for much of the off season.

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:25.320
- We really both listen
to each other's calls.

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:27.570
We kind of have what's
going on in the mornings

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:29.520
after workouts and when the
boys are doing breakfast,

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:30.960
we'll be like, "Okay,
what's your plan for today?

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:32.070
How many calls do you have?

00:12:32.070 --> 00:12:33.150
When are your calls?

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:34.560
Are there ones that I can listen in on?

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:35.700
Are there ones that he's
like, you know what,

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:36.870
I wanna, I wanna be a part of that,

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:39.930
I wanna participate or just listen in."

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:41.160
- [Jason] The two also work together

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:42.990
on the Suh Family Foundation,

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:44.970
the charitable arm of
Suh's business ventures

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:47.700
that focuses on empowering
people with resources

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:49.500
for long term success.

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:52.653
One issue they're working to
address is financial literacy.

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:58.860
- Right now, there is no
financial literacy requirement

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:00.570
at all at any level.

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:02.160
And to me that's mind blowing.

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:05.310
It, finances are in our life
from the minute we are born

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:06.960
to the minute we are no longer here.

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:11.280
And so to not have a
requirement of how things run,

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:14.070
how to properly use your finances,

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:17.070
is in, is to me quite insane.

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:20.280
We have specific programs that
we work with that students

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:22.830
that are coming from all
different types of backgrounds

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:26.820
are able to come together
and really learn.

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:28.380
How that's structured, right now,

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:29.970
we're focused on our own backyard,

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:31.260
which would be Portland, Oregon.

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:34.623
The ultimate goal would
be to be in every state.

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:36.450
- [Jason] And while many athletes

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:37.590
give back to their community,

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:38.910
The Suh's extend that notion

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:41.790
to all of their business
enterprises as well,

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:44.610
looking not only at how they
can impact the bottom line,

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:46.530
but impact their communities.

00:13:46.530 --> 00:13:48.933
And one of those spaces
has been restaurants.

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:01.983
- Hi.
- Hi.

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:06.270
- [Jason] One of Suh's
restaurant investments

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:07.920
is here in Downtown Portland.

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:10.230
A fast casual restaurant
called Baes Fried Chicken,

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:12.300
he started with a previously successful

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:15.750
Portland-based restaurateur
named Micah Camden.

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:19.110
To understand it, I ordered a
chicken sandwich for myself,

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:20.043
for journalism.

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:23.850
Tell me about where we are.

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:25.380
- We are in Downtown Portland.

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:27.570
This building was built in 1882.

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:29.040
So that's an old building.

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:31.380
You don't get much older
than that in Portland.

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:32.430
We got the fried chicken sandwich,

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:33.270
we got the chicken strips.

00:14:33.270 --> 00:14:36.810
It's easy, it's approachable,
it's super great quality.

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:37.643
When we opened this up,

00:14:37.643 --> 00:14:39.120
we went straight organic
on all the chicken,

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:39.953
which was awesome.

00:14:39.953 --> 00:14:43.080
- It's very, very juicy, so to be careful.

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:45.000
- [Jason] But good food
and a good location

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:48.060
aren't guarantees of success
in the restaurant world.

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:50.040
Restaurants are tricky
businesses for athletes,

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:51.690
seemingly easy to understand,

00:14:51.690 --> 00:14:54.660
but complex to conceive,
operate and market.

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:57.210
Actually, they're hard
businesses for anyone,

00:14:57.210 --> 00:14:58.920
even food pros.

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:01.080
While Micah himself has
had successful ventures

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:02.730
with restaurants like Boxer Ramen,

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:05.280
Little Big Burger, and Blue Star Donut,

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:08.370
in 2016 he had to shut
down a previous attempt

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:09.870
at a fried chicken restaurant.

00:15:11.130 --> 00:15:12.690
- [Micah] Restaurants are high risk.

00:15:12.690 --> 00:15:13.523
- [Jason] What's appealing to you

00:15:13.523 --> 00:15:16.020
about being in this particular
restaurant business?

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:17.550
- It's one, this is a foodie town,

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:18.630
and they got amazing chefs,

00:15:18.630 --> 00:15:22.050
people that enjoy quality,
high quality food,

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:24.390
and kind of giving back to my community

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:25.530
is something that was missing.

00:15:25.530 --> 00:15:28.530
And then also you come out
and see during a pandemic,

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:31.050
a lot of people are supporting
people in their endeavors

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:32.550
and seeing the success that they're doing

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:33.483
and wanna support people

00:15:33.483 --> 00:15:35.640
that when they're sometimes
in their rough spots

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:37.500
or even when they're at their heights.

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:38.333
- [Jason] The last few years

00:15:38.333 --> 00:15:39.600
have been far from the salad days

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:41.280
for the restaurant industry.

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:44.040
The pandemic altered dining,
living and working habits,

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:46.560
and everything from
lockdowns to labor shortages

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:48.510
have plagued the food business.

00:15:48.510 --> 00:15:49.770
Baes opened this location

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:51.570
only a few months before
the global pandemic

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:53.340
shut down much of the country,

00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:55.680
and George Floyd's
murder led to a reckoning

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:57.390
around racial inequality.

00:15:57.390 --> 00:16:00.210
As protests and counter
protests swept the country,

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:02.460
Portland became a focal point.

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:05.460
Images of property
destruction by protestors,

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:08.370
who themselves were often the
victims of violent attacks

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:12.060
by extreme right wing groups,
as well as Portland police,

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:14.220
ricocheted across social media,

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:17.130
giving rise to a flood of
disinformation by opponents

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:18.690
of police reform.

00:16:18.690 --> 00:16:19.950
- [Micah] This was ground zero,

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:21.450
right out our front door was ground zero

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:22.500
for the nightly protests

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:24.630
that went on for three or four months.

00:16:24.630 --> 00:16:27.090
Thankfully enough, this
community knew that this,

00:16:27.090 --> 00:16:29.880
this location was owned by Suh

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:32.790
and then we didn't get stuff
thrown through our windows,

00:16:32.790 --> 00:16:33.987
you know, stuff like that.

00:16:33.987 --> 00:16:36.810
But the attention that
was brought to Portland

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:38.430
via those protests,

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:39.990
it hit its cause, it hit its nerve,

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:41.760
and it started the conversation

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:43.860
that is still going on today.

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:45.660
- [Jason] Portland has in recent years,

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:49.620
been an almost cartoonish
example of a liberal stronghold.

00:16:49.620 --> 00:16:52.680
And while coffee shops and
breweries are a thing here,

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:54.780
the real Portland is a complicated place

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:57.360
in a state of near existential crisis,

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:00.963
facing issues around affordability,
the unhoused and crime.

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:03.030
People of color in Portland

00:17:03.030 --> 00:17:05.370
have been historically disenfranchised,

00:17:05.370 --> 00:17:08.370
especially when it comes to one
of Suh's biggest businesses,

00:17:08.370 --> 00:17:10.650
real estate and development.

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:13.800
Like in many cities across
the US, nearly a century ago,

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:16.230
redlining practices
allowed white neighborhoods

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:17.940
easier access to capital

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:20.610
and excluded black and
immigrant communities,

00:17:20.610 --> 00:17:22.800
including Suh's home in
the Northeast of Portland

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:25.350
where Irvington was,
quote, still desirable.

00:17:25.350 --> 00:17:29.460
Just a few blocks north, Alberta
was considered hazardous.

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:32.220
And while redlining was
made illegal in the 60s,

00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:34.890
its effects are still being felt.

00:17:34.890 --> 00:17:36.900
Investment in areas like Alberta,

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:38.850
where Suh and his father bought a property

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:41.070
have helped revitalize neighborhoods,

00:17:41.070 --> 00:17:43.230
but it's also led to gentrification,

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:47.310
driving up home prices and
pushing people of color out.

00:17:47.310 --> 00:17:49.320
Through his development company, HMS,

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:52.143
Suh is hoping to change
the traditional narrative.

00:17:57.060 --> 00:18:00.060
- We are down on Alberta,

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:01.650
further down from where we were at before,

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:05.670
but this is 30th, where we
are creating Alberta Alley,

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:10.670
which is going to be a
nice collection of BIPOC

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:12.990
type of restaurants.

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:15.300
- [Jason] Beyond creating a
space for black, indigenous,

00:18:15.300 --> 00:18:17.730
and people of color owners of restaurants,

00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:20.610
Suh has also brought in
more black investors,

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:23.940
including another pro football
player with Portland roots

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:25.410
named Brennan Scarlett.

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:26.730
We went to take a look,

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:30.180
but as you might expect,
we had to go inside.

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:31.500
Let's go inside.

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:32.730
- I know, we got our Portland bad weather-

00:18:32.730 --> 00:18:34.530
- [Jason] And get out
of this Portland rain.

00:18:34.530 --> 00:18:36.360
Right now, Alberta
Alley is still very much

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:37.590
a work in progress,

00:18:37.590 --> 00:18:39.300
but the intention is to create a space

00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:40.950
for multiple restaurants and eateries

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:44.220
in a communal, well,
alley-type experience.

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:46.710
Currently they're working to
bring a Latino-owned expansion

00:18:46.710 --> 00:18:50.190
of Papi's Churros and another
Baes Fried Chicken location

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:51.093
to this space.

00:18:52.680 --> 00:18:55.020
You guys are native sons, you know,

00:18:55.020 --> 00:19:00.020
sort of putting a different
mark on this city.

00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:01.650
Tell me about, you know,

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:06.120
your process of being an
owner and not just a like,

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:09.180
participant, not just a
customer to some extent.

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:11.460
- Yeah, Portland's an interesting place

00:19:11.460 --> 00:19:15.180
and has a long history of racism,

00:19:15.180 --> 00:19:18.360
of marginalizing certain groups.

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:20.190
Like the house that I grew up in,

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:21.990
on the deed, it says that a black person

00:19:21.990 --> 00:19:23.550
couldn't own that house, right.

00:19:23.550 --> 00:19:25.957
But my dad, you know,
purchased that house back,

00:19:25.957 --> 00:19:27.960
you know, in the 90s.

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:29.490
Right now you think about like

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:32.340
purchasing commercial
properties like this, right,

00:19:32.340 --> 00:19:33.900
under black ownership.

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:38.190
And then also, you know, the
cycle of having tenants here

00:19:38.190 --> 00:19:40.920
and being intentional
about having tenants here

00:19:40.920 --> 00:19:42.120
that are represented,

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:44.187
that may have historically
not been able to have

00:19:44.187 --> 00:19:45.887
the opportunity to be represented.

00:19:46.890 --> 00:19:47.850
- [Suh] When I grew up here,

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:49.860
it was predominantly a black neighborhood

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:51.640
and a lot of different people

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:54.600
just kind of starting
out their businesses here

00:19:54.600 --> 00:19:56.700
that really in the BIPOC space.

00:19:56.700 --> 00:19:59.790
And so a lot of gentrification
moved those things out

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:01.440
and brought up new developments

00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:03.360
and not really looking for a way

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:04.740
to bring those people back in.

00:20:04.740 --> 00:20:07.560
And so this is an interesting
way to fold them back into

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:09.960
having that opportunity of
kind of getting kicked out

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:12.480
and now coming back and
hopefully being successful

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:15.210
and finding ways to
help them be successful.

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:18.000
We have a very altruistic view

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:20.730
when it comes to our real estate endeavors

00:20:20.730 --> 00:20:22.950
and have multifamily units

00:20:22.950 --> 00:20:26.640
and be able to create them
in mixed use developments.

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:28.680
I think one of the most
things I'm gonna be proud of

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:31.350
as we're getting this journey

00:20:31.350 --> 00:20:33.330
and started is our ally project

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:34.860
to attainable living initiative,

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:37.980
where we really want to
have the ability to afford

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:40.140
attainable living for everybody.

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:41.315
And we're starting here in Portland.

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:44.700
- There's never been a
greater need for housing

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:46.890
and yet it's almost impossible

00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:49.050
to get housing done and developed.

00:20:49.050 --> 00:20:52.080
- [Jason] Suh's partner
at HMS is Joel Anderson,

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:54.390
a third generation
contractor and developer

00:20:54.390 --> 00:20:55.860
based in Portland.

00:20:55.860 --> 00:20:57.750
And while the two have
developed a portfolio

00:20:57.750 --> 00:21:01.140
of everything from high-rise
residential to industrial,

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:03.030
one of the common threads is their vision

00:21:03.030 --> 00:21:05.613
of the social impact real estate can have.

00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:08.460
- Both Suh and I have been very fortunate

00:21:08.460 --> 00:21:09.390
and worked very hard.

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:11.430
Some lucky bounces that that win our way.

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:13.500
And there's a whole bunch of people

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:15.270
that we interact with in our communities

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:17.160
that just didn't have
those same opportunities

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:18.990
and have never had access

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:21.300
to invest in commercial real estate.

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:22.530
- [Jason] While real estate investments

00:21:22.530 --> 00:21:24.480
have been a great place to store wealth,

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:25.770
the recent housing market

00:21:25.770 --> 00:21:28.290
has left many unable to participate,

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:31.440
especially when it comes
to BIPOC communities.

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:33.207
- And I think one of the
ways that we can do that

00:21:33.207 --> 00:21:35.100
with alt cap, as we call it,

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:38.280
is giving them the opportunity
to not only pay rent

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:40.710
and understand those different
pieces and keep it afloat,

00:21:40.710 --> 00:21:42.960
but also give them the
opportunity to be a part of it.

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:44.250
And one thing you know about people,

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:47.280
when they have ownership into a building,

00:21:47.280 --> 00:21:49.322
they take care of it
that much more better.

00:21:49.322 --> 00:21:50.580
- [News Broadcaster] All
of these people are really-

00:21:50.580 --> 00:21:52.860
- [Jason] Affordable housing,
which about half of Americans

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:54.570
say their community lacks,

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:58.020
is a hot button issue long
freighted with racism.

00:21:58.020 --> 00:22:01.200
Some say they oppose it over
perceived increases in crime,

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:04.710
erosion in the tax base,
and increased traffic.

00:22:04.710 --> 00:22:07.740
Suh and Joel believe they can
ultimately flip the script

00:22:07.740 --> 00:22:11.250
by better knitting residents
into the community economically

00:22:11.250 --> 00:22:13.650
by affording everyone a stake.

00:22:13.650 --> 00:22:16.290
- Altruistic capital, alt cap,

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:19.590
it's very intention is to bring people in

00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:20.700
that have been excluded.

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:22.590
If we take an accredited investor,

00:22:22.590 --> 00:22:23.423
somebody and say,

00:22:23.423 --> 00:22:25.530
"Okay, you're gonna invest
a $100,000 in this project."

00:22:25.530 --> 00:22:26.430
They go, "Yes."

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:29.340
We go, "But you're only gonna
get credit for 95% of that."

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:31.860
And $5,000 or 5% of your investment

00:22:31.860 --> 00:22:34.020
is gonna go to a non-profit

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:35.760
alt cap community reinvestment fund.

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:38.490
And we say to them now,
"Okay, you can participate

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:40.920
from 500 to $5,000,

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:42.960
and for every dollar you invest,

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:45.060
we will match you through
the alt cap reinvestment fund

00:22:45.060 --> 00:22:45.960
dollar for dollar."

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:48.870
And so if you have
somebody who invests $500,

00:22:48.870 --> 00:22:51.120
they now have a thousand
dollars investment

00:22:51.120 --> 00:22:51.953
into this project.

00:22:51.953 --> 00:22:54.300
They've effectively doubled their return.

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:55.500
- [Jason] It may seem complicated,

00:22:55.500 --> 00:22:58.560
but Joel and Suh believe
that programs like alt cap

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:01.920
can give people who've long
been excluded from real estate,

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:04.230
an attainable investment option.

00:23:04.230 --> 00:23:05.670
While some investors might balk

00:23:05.670 --> 00:23:07.590
at giving up some of their economics,

00:23:07.590 --> 00:23:09.810
proponents of this shared ownership

00:23:09.810 --> 00:23:12.000
believe it's long overdue.

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:14.640
- If we can get real estate transactions

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:17.040
to include everybody,

00:23:17.040 --> 00:23:20.190
you're talking about a
trillion dollar industry

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:24.660
that has the ability to engage
everybody in a micro fashion

00:23:24.660 --> 00:23:26.400
earned at a disproportionate rate,

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:29.302
you can transition the wealth
of our country in short order.

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:32.820
- [Jason] Of course, undoing centuries

00:23:32.820 --> 00:23:36.060
of economic disparity and
systemic financial bias,

00:23:36.060 --> 00:23:37.530
that's a long game.

00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:39.870
For Suh, it's his life's work

00:23:39.870 --> 00:23:42.360
and he'll be able to
devote even more time to it

00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:44.970
once he leaves the game of football.

00:23:44.970 --> 00:23:46.290
When you leave the League,

00:23:46.290 --> 00:23:49.230
how do you think about
the idea of transition,

00:23:49.230 --> 00:23:51.750
especially given everything

00:23:51.750 --> 00:23:53.820
that you've already done in business?

00:23:53.820 --> 00:23:56.635
- [Suh] Yeah, the transition's
a difficult one for me.

00:23:56.635 --> 00:23:59.400
I look at it consistent and
have over the last couple years

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:00.240
from a standpoint,

00:24:00.240 --> 00:24:02.190
especially since the
boys came into my life,

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:03.810
I wanna spend as much time with them

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:05.910
and not miss out on things.

00:24:05.910 --> 00:24:07.740
- [Jason] How do you
balance like the economics

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:08.790
versus the emotions?

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:10.620
I mean, this is a deep analysis.

00:24:10.620 --> 00:24:11.970
This is one of the most
important decisions

00:24:11.970 --> 00:24:12.803
you'll ever make.

00:24:12.803 --> 00:24:15.900
- [Suh] I think the analysis
is very straightforward.

00:24:15.900 --> 00:24:17.820
It comes down to numbers in a lot of ways,

00:24:17.820 --> 00:24:21.030
but then also just do I
believe I have more to offer?

00:24:21.030 --> 00:24:22.020
Without question.

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:24.150
And do I aspire to be in the hall of fame?

00:24:24.150 --> 00:24:24.983
No question.

00:24:24.983 --> 00:24:26.100
Do I want to do that at the cost

00:24:26.100 --> 00:24:27.540
of not spending time with my kids,

00:24:27.540 --> 00:24:29.250
not being around my family?

00:24:29.250 --> 00:24:32.130
Timing is always gonna be key to that.

00:24:32.130 --> 00:24:33.960
We've got a good process
with me and the wife.

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:37.530
I lean on her a lot and I
think she has a good telltale

00:24:37.530 --> 00:24:39.540
to give me some different
things to think about.

00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:41.340
And she always says it's
ultimately your decision,

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:43.893
but I truthfully know it's
both of our decisions.

